00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool,
so we have you here today

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:38.950
because we wanted to discuss
a little bit about TensorFlow.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:41.220
But before we start
talking about TensorFlow,

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:45.550
for all the audience that
have never done any machine

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:50.150
learning, what is-- TensorFlow,
I've heard, is neural networks.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.100
What is a neural network?

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.526
ELI BIXBY: Yeah, so TensorFlow
is mainly for neural networks.

00:04:57.526 --> 00:05:02.050
A neural network
in general is, you

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:05.550
can think of it sort of like
as a directed graph, where

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:12.350
the nodes are simply arithmetic
activations of their inputs.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.790
So there are some
nodes upstream,

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:17.260
they feed into a
downstream node,

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:22.090
and the node has a single output
based on the upstream nodes.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.310
And it was sort of
discovered a long time ago,

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.370
like in the '70s, that
you could use these

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.400
and you could use
back propagation,

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.570
which just basically says, I
want the top layer of nodes

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:37.540
to have this certain-- or
I want the output layer

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:39.640
to have a certain output
when the top layer has

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.200
a certain input.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.700
And you can use back propagation
to adjust the function

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:50.450
that each node operates on to
line up with these outputs,

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:52.640
and that you can model
very complex functions

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:56.040
with many layers
of neural networks.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.840
MARK MANDEL: Where did
this idea come from?

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.160
Do you know any of the history
of how it sort of came about?

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.160
ELI BIXBY: I'm not sure.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.840
I think it was
actually originally

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:12.080
sort of a theoretical attempt
to model human cognition, which

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.760
is why it's neural networks.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:20.470
I'm not sure that that
is part of its usage now,

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:23.520
but I think that's where
the name came from.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Cool, so just to try

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.630
to understand a bit a better
what neural networks are,

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:29.171
basically, at the
end of the day what

00:06:29.171 --> 00:06:31.300
you have is a bunch of
nodes, and every note is

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:34.127
like a function where you
get bunch of parameters

00:06:34.127 --> 00:06:34.710
and an output.

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.501
ELI BIXBY: Yeah, and
the nodes are actually

00:06:36.501 --> 00:06:38.760
really a helpful
extract abstraction

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:40.860
for us to think about
it, but it's actually--

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:45.280
TensorFlow is named TensorFlow
because its tensor is flowing

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:45.970
along a graph.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:49.290
And really, you think
of it as the layers

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:53.990
are single entities that are
tensor transformations from one

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:55.880
to the next layer.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:59.800
And so often we talk about
layers and the activation

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:03.170
function of a layer
of the tensor graph

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:04.860
rather than individual nodes.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Cool, So now we know

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:11.700
what TensorFlow
comes from, the name.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.740
Why is TensorFlow a thing?

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.490
ELI BIXBY: Yeah,
so TensorFlow is

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:21.590
the successor to Google's
previous machine learning

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:22.880
framework.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.850
And really, the reason
it's open sourced

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:30.900
is we believe that there is
going to be a lot of innovation

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:33.840
in machine learning,
and we think

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.450
it's important for these tools
to sort of be standardized

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:41.480
and for work to be shared among
the machine learning community.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:46.484
And so having a common
framework for expressing machine

00:07:46.484 --> 00:07:48.150
learning algorithms
is really important.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:50.650
So previously you'd see papers,
and you'd see people kind of

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:53.770
informally describe
the algorithm

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:56.030
that they were writing
about, but now you

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:58.880
can actually publish
the code and it's

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:00.820
another step towards
reproducible research,

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.390
it allows people to
collaborate more easily,

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:05.916
all the great things we
love about open source.

00:08:05.916 --> 00:08:07.290
MARK MANDEL: So
you touched on it

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:09.380
a little bit when you started
talking about tensors flowing

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:12.110
through a graph, but can we
break that down even further?

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:13.200
What's a tensor?

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:14.440
How does this work?

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:16.440
Give us sort of like a
base level understanding.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:17.740
ELI BIXBY: Yeah,
so mathematicians

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:19.448
are going to hate me
for this definition,

00:08:19.448 --> 00:08:24.290
but a tensor is a
multi-dimensional matrix.

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.970
So you think of a vector as
just a list of numbers, right?

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:30.790
So like an array in Python.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:34.360
And a matrix, then, is a
list of lists of numbers,

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:39.470
and so a tensor is-- a vector
is one dimensional tensor,

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:41.750
a matrix is a two
dimensional tensor,

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:46.100
and then a matrix of vectors
is a three dimensional tensor.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:50.620
And you can expand that
and you get this concept

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:54.250
of shape, which is a
vector that represents

00:08:54.250 --> 00:08:56.190
the shape of a tensor.

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:58.310
And so for the users
who've used NumPy

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:02.650
before this should be a
very familiar concept.

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:05.430
TensorFlow actually follows
the exact same syntax

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:08.660
that NumPy does for
defining these things.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:12.212
MARK MANDEL: And so how
does that correlate into how

00:09:12.212 --> 00:09:13.170
we do machine learning?

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:14.570
Like, why is that important?

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:17.945
ELI BIXBY: Yeah, so
it's really important

00:09:17.945 --> 00:09:19.820
in particular for deep
learning because a lot

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:21.590
of these algorithms
can be thought

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:24.480
of as a series of
tensor computations.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:28.680
And there's a lot
of optimization

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.810
you can also do restricting
the data that's flowing along

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:32.739
your graph to tensors.

00:09:32.739 --> 00:09:34.780
So if you compare it with
something like a Apache

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:39.051
Beam or Spark, where there's
sort of arbitrary data flowing

00:09:39.051 --> 00:09:40.550
along your computation
graph and you

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:42.120
can define your
own serialization

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.600
and de-serialization,
TensorFlow, by contrast,

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:50.712
is all in memory and
calls out to very fast see

00:09:50.712 --> 00:09:54.640
implementations of
these tensor operations.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:58.930
And obviously, that allows
you to plug into Cuda

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:01.610
and use GPUs really easily.

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:06.086
So restricting it to tensors
gets us a lot of functionality.

00:10:06.086 --> 00:10:07.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Interesting, so you actually

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:12.250
mention GPUs, because I'm
assuming that GPUs are better

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.360
suited for these
kind of competitions,

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:17.444
but we also announced
this other thing, TPUs.

00:10:17.444 --> 00:10:19.110
ELI BIXBY: Yeah, so
the strength of GPUs

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:20.850
actually ties a
lot into why we're

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:23.400
seeing a resurgence
in neural networks,

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:26.270
is the techniques have
been around for a long time

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:28.580
and now we have this
commodity hardware that's just

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:31.110
fantastic for neural networks.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:34.790
And we're seeing neural
networks be extremely useful

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:38.240
in all of these
applications, and so we're

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:42.750
seeing a huge growth, and we're
actually getting to the point

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:44.750
where it makes sense to
have actually have ASICs

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:46.200
for TensorFlow.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.780
So that's what a TPU is.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:50.000
MARK MANDEL: What's an ASIC?

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.830
ELI BIXBY: An ASIC is, I think
the acronym is application

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:56.000
specific integrated chip.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:59.510
So it's a custom
piece of hardware

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:03.130
that-- and the optimization
you're looking for,

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:05.800
because we have these big
data centers, the optimization

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:08.550
you're looking for is
performance per watt.

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:12.530
So they allow you
to do the operations

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:17.120
that are common in machine
learning at a lower power,

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:19.820
and that means you get a
higher throughput because you

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:23.652
can run a lot more of them for
the same amount of electricity.

00:11:23.652 --> 00:11:25.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Cool, so it sounds

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:29.030
like TensorFlow is kind of a
complicated and pretty complex

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:30.690
framework.

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:33.060
Does that mean that
to get started with it

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:36.770
I need to understand what
tensors and monads are?

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:42.130
ELI BIXBY: No, so that the
actual goal is that developers

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:44.642
can start writing machine
learning algorithms

00:11:44.642 --> 00:11:46.600
and start incorporating
machine learning easily

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:51.200
into their code, and that's
a huge goal of TensorFlow.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:54.790
So TensorFlow itself, there's
a lot you can do with it.

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.612
You can write custom operations.

00:11:57.612 --> 00:11:59.320
You can fiddle around
with the internals,

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:02.547
but we try to provide a high
level interface for people

00:12:02.547 --> 00:12:04.130
who don't necessarily
want to do that,

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:07.430
and we're actually looking at
building more layers on top

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:10.680
of TensorFlow to make
it easier for developers

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:11.720
to get started with it.

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:16.290
So I think it's
publicly available,

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:19.580
but not-- I think
it's like whatever

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:22.480
the open source version of
alpha would be considered.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:25.500
We have a package called
Wide and Deep which

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.090
provides a higher level
abstraction on top

00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:30.080
of TensorFlow that
allows you to say,

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:33.930
I have sparse features,
learn these sparse features.

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:37.510
Or, I have dense features,
learn these dense features.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:40.020
And so it's very--
all you really

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:42.110
have to know about is
what your data looks like.

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:43.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, cool.

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:47.140
So how do I know if
TensorFlow is good for me?

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:50.650
What are the typical use
cases where the answer

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:51.910
is that, oh yeah, TensorFlow.

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:55.830
ELI BIXBY: Yeah, so
we hope to expand it

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.850
to a lot of different machine
learning applications.

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:02.470
I think in particular if
you have a problem that's

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:06.010
well-suited to deep
learning, TensorFlow

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:10.020
is a great solution for you.

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:11.590
Again, deep learning
algorithms, they

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:14.160
require a huge amount
of data to train,

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:17.490
more so than many other
machine learning algorithms.

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:20.070
So you need to have that
at least somewhat available

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:20.570
to you.

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:22.070
MARK MANDEL: What's
a good heuristic

00:13:22.070 --> 00:13:23.870
for a large amount of data?

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:30.460
ELI BIXBY: Yeah, I think
the ballpark lower level is

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:40.215
maybe like 100,000 labeled is
common, for example, for text.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:45.820
Yeah, text or image.

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:48.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so
you mentioned this label.

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:52.170
Does that mean that your
data needs to be labeled?

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:54.307
Actually, what does it
mean to be labeled and do

00:13:54.307 --> 00:13:54.890
you need that?

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:59.230
ELI BIXBY: So we
commonly break down usage

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:03.160
of deep learning into two
different parts, training

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:04.320
and inference.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:10.220
And so training is running
this TensorFlow graph

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:16.450
on your label data, and using it
to generate the proper weights.

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:19.050
And then inferences, you
have this unlabeled data

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:22.620
where you want to
predict the label,

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:27.320
and so you're giving that to
the trained TensorFlow graph

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:30.511
and it's spitting out a
probability for the label.

00:14:30.511 --> 00:14:32.010
MARK MANDEL: So
when you say a label

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:34.260
it's kind of like, all right,
I've got a bunch of data

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:36.739
wherein, I don't
know, pick an example.

00:14:36.739 --> 00:14:38.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You
have a bunch of emails

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:40.410
and you need to know
if it's spam or not.

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:40.890
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, that works.

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:42.020
That's great.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:44.540
So you know whether it's
spam, and so you can feed that

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:45.960
through the system and be
like, yes, these are spam,

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:46.490
these aren't.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:47.990
And it could be like,
yay, now I'm learning.

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:50.130
And then at the end when
you're doing your inference

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:52.190
you can hand it an e-mail and
it will tell you whether or not

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:54.420
it's spam with I guess
some degree of certainty.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:56.253
ELI BIXBY: Yeah, e-mail
is a perfect example

00:14:56.253 --> 00:14:59.160
because then you have a bunch of
users essentially providing you

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:00.000
with labels.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.890
They click the
Report Spam button

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:07.460
and, bam, you have another
labeled piece of data.

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:11.470
And there's increasing
levels of sophistication,

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:14.450
but that's sort of the basics
is like, I have this label,

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:17.290
I want to predict the
label of something else.

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:19.930
So maybe you have a confidence
cut off and you're like,

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:22.930
oh, my model is not very
confident about this piece

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:25.650
of spam so I'm going to
surface it to the user.

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You were
mentioning something before,

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:29.340
which I thought
it was very cool.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:32.500
It was the one with
Reddit sub-Reddits.

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:37.680
ELI BIXBY: Yes, so we gave we
gave a talk at OSCON and PyCon

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:41.730
where we made a little
example algorithm based

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:45.820
on some colleagues' work
with tax classification,

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:49.780
and we used it to
classify Reddit post

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:52.320
titles by sub-Reddit.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.400
And we got, I think, a
pretty good accuracy.

00:15:54.400 --> 00:16:00.530
Not state of the art, but
I think it was mid 90s.

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:04.440
And yeah, these
are topics that are

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:07.590
very well suited because there's
large data sets available.

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:11.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So let's talk
about that specific example.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:15.480
How does it look for a
developer to do that?

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:17.190
Imagine that I
have all the titles

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:19.640
and what sub-Reddit
they belong to.

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:20.730
What is the next step?

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:22.250
ELI BIXBY: Yeah,
so there's going

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:27.700
to be a pre-processing
set up, and we frequently

00:16:27.700 --> 00:16:31.080
use Apache Beam to
pre-process our data.

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:32.830
And so then once
you have your data

00:16:32.830 --> 00:16:37.460
in sort of this
nice vector format,

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:42.180
you can go and grab and try
to adapt for your use case

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:45.200
one of the many open source--
there's many models already

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:46.370
out there.

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:49.730
So frequently, you'll try
to figure out, what category

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:51.050
does my problem fit into?

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:54.070
OK, it's a text
classification problem.

00:16:54.070 --> 00:16:57.800
Oh great, someone's already
written a text classification

00:16:57.800 --> 00:16:58.740
model.

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:01.740
And then you'll try training
that model on your data

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:03.940
with your pre-process
data, and then

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:06.220
you'll do some testing
and some validation

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:08.010
and say, OK this worked,
this didn't work,

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:10.859
let me tinker around with
the model a little bit

00:17:10.859 --> 00:17:13.550
until you get sort of an
accuracy you're happy with.

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:14.880
MARK MANDEL: Let's sort of
take an even further step back.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:15.940
OK, so you say model.

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:19.284
What is does that input and
output look like with a model?

00:17:19.284 --> 00:17:20.450
How does that work together?

00:17:20.450 --> 00:17:21.908
ELI BIXBY: So
TensorFlow graphs are

00:17:21.908 --> 00:17:23.339
defined with this Python code.

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:26.170
So TensorFlow is like
a Python package.

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:30.720
You write operations,
and an operation

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:32.210
will typically
have arguments that

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:34.050
are previous operations
you've defined.

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:37.790
So in this way, you're
defining the directed graph.

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:39.467
So there's two parts.

00:17:39.467 --> 00:17:41.050
There's the graph
definition, and then

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:43.690
there's the training step
where you're running through,

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:47.160
basically, a loop saying, OK,
grab a batch of my training

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:51.130
data, run it through the graph.

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:53.150
It'll calculate gradients.

00:17:53.150 --> 00:17:57.560
It will update the
operations on each node,

00:17:57.560 --> 00:18:00.000
like we were talking about.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.759
And then do that
until we're satisfied

00:18:02.759 --> 00:18:04.300
with whatever accuracy
we're getting,

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:06.030
or whatever loss we're getting.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:10.920
Whatever function we've
decided is our metric.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:13.570
And then that will
constitute a model.

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:16.491
And then there are also lots
of [INAUDIBLE] or packages

00:18:16.491 --> 00:18:17.490
that we've open-sourced.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:19.520
So for example,
TensorFlow serving

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:21.960
allows you to serve models.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:25.240
So do inference,
that's the other half.

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:27.750
So you'll export a
model, which is exporting

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:30.890
these weights and
this graph to a file,

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:33.660
and then TensorFlow serving
can load it for you,

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:37.600
and then provide you
an API, a gRPC API

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:42.250
where you can say-- you
can call it with a vector

00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:43.829
and it will return
back the output

00:18:43.829 --> 00:18:44.870
vector that you'd expect.

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:46.674
Maybe the labels.

00:18:46.674 --> 00:18:49.090
MARK MANDEL: So if I was taking,
say, like a Reddit title,

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:51.720
and I'm going to convert
that into a vector, what

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:53.020
does that vector look like?

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:57.004
ELI BIXBY: Yeah, so that's--
I talked a little bit about

00:18:57.004 --> 00:18:58.420
before, without
explaining myself,

00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:02.550
about sparse data
and dense data.

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:06.400
So like a Reddit--
maybe a Reddit title

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:10.680
is a list of vectors
where each vector is

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:13.160
your entire vocabulary.

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:15.600
So maybe 40,000 words
in the English language,

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:19.620
or whatever, and
there's a 1 somewhere

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:23.810
in there corresponding to
the word cat, is a one.

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:27.710
And maybe cat is like
the 30,250th word.

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:31.120
So like that 30,250th index
in your vector is a 1,

00:19:31.120 --> 00:19:32.810
and everything else is 0s.

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:36.140
So that's sparse data.

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:40.890
Frequently in deep learning
we'll do embeddings.

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:46.350
So we'll try to learn denser
representations of data,

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:49.200
because the neural nets
tend to handle these denser

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:51.960
representations better.

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:57.950
So, and this is something we
talked about in our workshop,

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:00.480
words actually lend
themselves very well

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:02.590
to this dense embedding.

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:05.170
So you may actually
already have a model.

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:07.280
So there may be,
if you look at-- so

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:09.700
we open sourced
SyntaxNet, for example,

00:20:09.700 --> 00:20:14.310
which is a piece of Google's
natural language processing

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:15.295
written in TensorFlow.

00:20:15.295 --> 00:20:17.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But wasn't
it called Parsey McParseface?

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:19.940
ELI BIXBY: Yeah, so there's
a subtle difference here.

00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:21.560
We open sourced two things.

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:25.330
We open sourced SyntaxNet,
which is the graph definition,

00:20:25.330 --> 00:20:28.280
and then we open sourced a
trained model, so including

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:32.660
the weights and all
the trained bits,

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:34.230
called Parsey
McParceface which was

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:37.350
trained on a subset of
the data that we have.

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:39.320
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:41.570
ELI BIXBY: And so if
you look at SyntaxNet,

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:45.320
it's actually a bunch
of trained pieces,

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:48.120
a bunch of different neural
nets that feed into each other.

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:52.614
So there's this sense
of composability, which

00:20:52.614 --> 00:20:53.780
brings me back to embedding.

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:56.240
So you may have a model that
somebody else has already

00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:58.930
written for generating
word embeddings, where

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:03.880
you feed in your one hot vector
and it gives you back a denser

00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:05.180
representation of words.

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:08.030
So maybe what you're
learning is a mapping

00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:12.320
from these word embeddings
to sub-Reddit classification.

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:17.730
And so that's part of what we
hope to spur by this ecosystem

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:21.810
is this sense of
composability and other people

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:24.220
taking their models
and using them

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:27.960
as pieces of their own
models, and then providing

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:30.090
those models as pieces
for other people to use,

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:34.770
and just sort of building
this whole ecosystem.

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:38.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so
we've been talking models,

00:21:38.030 --> 00:21:42.251
and that basically you're going
to be training some neural net

00:21:42.251 --> 00:21:44.250
where you create that
model, and then that model

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:47.370
is a valuable piece of
information that you can use.

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:49.940
How big is a model?

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:52.210
ELI BIXBY: Oh man, I think
it varies a huge amount.

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:55.970
I'm not even sure how well
I can give a ballpark.

00:21:55.970 --> 00:22:00.000
I think it depends a huge
amount on the variables,

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:00.780
like the size.

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:05.252
Like if we had a
vocabulary of 50 words,

00:22:05.252 --> 00:22:07.460
and we were doing-- or, not
a vocabulary of 50 words.

00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:11.330
If we were doing an embedding
in a vector of 50 length

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:13.670
that's going to be a much
smaller file than an embedding

00:22:13.670 --> 00:22:16.452
of a vector of 100 length.

00:22:16.452 --> 00:22:18.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Are we
talking gigabytes here?

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:18.910
Terabytes?

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:21.030
ELI BIXBY: I think we
can be talking gigabytes.

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:23.180
Smaller models I think
may be under that,

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:27.440
but definitely-- I think
probably not a terabyte.

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:29.659
MARK MANDEL: OK, so it's
going to be quite a--

00:22:29.659 --> 00:22:31.200
ELI BIXBY: On the
order of gigabytes.

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:33.570
MARK MANDEL: OK, so
if I want to sit down

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:35.820
and I'm like, all right,
this thing sounds super cool,

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:37.754
it sounds super
interesting, how would I

00:22:37.754 --> 00:22:38.920
get started with TensorFlow?

00:22:38.920 --> 00:22:40.050
Where is a good place to start?

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:41.466
ELI BIXBY: Yeah,
so TensorFlow.org

00:22:41.466 --> 00:22:43.560
has a bunch of great
tutorials that the brain

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:45.035
team has provided.

00:22:49.030 --> 00:22:51.540
MNIST is kind of the
hello world of TensorFlow.

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:53.280
So that's this problem
that's been around

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:56.100
for a long time where you
have handwritten digits,

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:59.940
and you want to classify
them to the correct number.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:03.490
And so I think that's sort of
the first place to get started,

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:05.640
and there's an
MNIST for beginners

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:07.990
and there's an MNIST
for ML experts.

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:13.510
So it'll walk you through what
is MNIST, why is it important,

00:23:13.510 --> 00:23:16.740
here's a little bit of the math
behind the state of the art

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:21.150
MNIST algorithms if
you're a beginner.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:24.240
And if not it will be just like,
here's TensorFlow and here's

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:26.370
how it corresponds to
the MNIST algorithm

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:27.820
that you're familiar with.

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:28.945
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:23:28.945 --> 00:23:31.160
Also, since you
mentioned that workshop

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:33.490
that you gave at
OSCON and PyCon,

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:35.610
that was like three
hours and it is online.

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:37.480
Could you recommend
that as an entry point

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:39.570
to someone that has
never done TensorFlow?

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:41.861
ELI BIXBY: Yeah, we tried to
make it pretty accessible.

00:23:41.861 --> 00:23:43.490
I think even three
hours, It's kind of

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:47.530
hard to fit in a
comprehensive intro,

00:23:47.530 --> 00:23:50.270
and so I think that
it's a little squashed.

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:53.500
But certainly you
should check that out.

00:23:53.500 --> 00:23:56.440
And even if it's just
for the resource slides,

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:58.220
we have a lot of
resource slides in there

00:23:58.220 --> 00:24:01.024
to sort of get you
started on your own.

00:24:01.024 --> 00:24:03.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, that
sounds very interesting.

00:24:03.190 --> 00:24:05.180
We're kind of
running out of time.

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:06.930
Is there anything else
that you would like

00:24:06.930 --> 00:24:09.300
to mention before we finish?

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:11.220
ELI BIXBY: Not off
the top of my head.

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:13.550
Definitely check out TensorFlow.

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:16.890
It's worth a look.

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:18.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: One
last question, actually.

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:21.560
What is the best use
case of TensorFlow?

00:24:21.560 --> 00:24:25.470
The most fun usage of
TensorFlow you've ever seen?

00:24:25.470 --> 00:24:30.890
ELI BIXBY: So I love DCGAN's,
which is some crazy acronym,

00:24:30.890 --> 00:24:34.870
but generative adversarial
network is the last bit.

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:37.270
So it's actually two
neural nets that are

00:24:37.270 --> 00:24:39.060
competing against each other.

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:41.820
One of them is
trying to classify

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:44.560
the output of the first
one and your training data,

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:47.620
and the other one is trying to
generate from noise things that

00:24:47.620 --> 00:24:49.080
look like your training data.

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:50.830
And so you're training
this neural network

00:24:50.830 --> 00:24:56.040
to trick this other
neural network, basically.

00:24:56.040 --> 00:25:01.090
And so you generate-- there's
a bunch of hilarious pictures

00:25:01.090 --> 00:25:06.350
in the paper and in the
GitHub of generated bedrooms,

00:25:06.350 --> 00:25:08.550
and there they're like
uncanny valley bedrooms.

00:25:08.550 --> 00:25:10.930
There are things that
look almost like bedrooms

00:25:10.930 --> 00:25:13.220
but there is something
wrong about them,

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:16.420
and there's generated
faces and they're

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:18.240
like uncanny valley faces.

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:19.381
They're almost faces.

00:25:19.381 --> 00:25:21.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, we're
going to add a link to that

00:25:21.630 --> 00:25:23.338
